// =====================================================================
// KETA COASTAL FLOOD PREDICTION -- PRODUCTION GEE SCRIPT v5.1
// Generated by build_pipeline_v5.py from v4.3. Do not edit by hand.
// Sentinel-1 SAR + FES2022 + ERA5 + CHIRPS + GPM IMERG + SMAP + SRTM
// Last updated: July 2026
//
// CHANGES FROM v4.2 -> v4.3 (robustness release; see CONFIG below):
//   The recurring defect class in this pipeline has been silent fallback
//   to dummy constants when a source is missing (this is what the
//   deprecated SMAP collection did to 11 events before v4.2 caught it).
//   The same pattern still existed for the Sentinel-1 baseline, and
//   several "constants" were actually derived from the current 23-event
//   catalogue, so they changed meaning whenever an event was added.
//
//   A. HARD-FAIL ON MISSING SENTINEL-1 (result-neutral when data present)
//      getFloodLabel() previously unmasked an empty S1 collection to
//      0 dB. VH over land is about -15 to -25 dB, so an empty baseline
//      made diff = during - 0 ~ -20 dB, far below any Otsu threshold:
//      the whole domain would be labelled FLOODED, silently. Events with
//      no baseline or no during-image now yield a fully masked flood
//      band, so sample() returns ZERO rows for them instead of garbage,
//      and a "*** FAIL" line is printed. A qa_s1_ok band is exported.
//
//   B. FROZEN CONSTANTS (result-neutral for the 23-event catalogue)
//      SPRING_RANGE_THRESHOLD and the compound_risk tide-range bounds
//      were the median / min / max of the current catalogue. Adding an
//      event silently re-scaled compound_risk and could re-classify the
//      spring flag of already-published events (verified: adding the
//      2015 and 2017 candidates together flips jun2023). They are now
//      pinned to their 2026-07 values and documented as fixed.
//
//   C. OPT-IN LABEL FIXES -- DEFAULT OFF (these CHANGE the labels)
//      MASK_WATER_BEFORE_OTSU and RFICII_PERCENTILES_TRAIN_ONLY correct
//      two real methodological issues, but switching either one on
//      regenerates the flood / driver labels and therefore invalidates
//      every published v4.2 number. They are OFF so this script
//      reproduces v4.2 byte-for-byte. Turn them on only for a v5 run
//      where the whole analysis is re-executed and re-reported.
//
// CHANGES FROM v4.1 -> v4.2:
// 1. FIXED (dataset): NASA_USDA/HSL/SMAP10KM_soil_moisture is DEPRECATED
//    in the GEE catalog (data ends 2022-08-02). Every event after Aug 2022
//    silently fell back to the 0.2 dummy constant. Replaced with
//    NASA/SMAP/SPL4SMGP/008 (3-hourly, 2015-03 to present), band
//    "sm_surface" (volumetric fraction 0-0.9). The 0.1-0.5 normalization
//    in compound_risk now operates on genuinely volumetric units.
// 2. FIXED (missing band): surface_pressure was announced in the v4.1
//    changelog but never implemented in getERA5(). It is now actually
//    selected from ERA5 HOURLY and exported (inverse-barometer proxy, Pa).
// 3. FIXED (data consistency): FES2022_DATA regenerated by
//    extract_fes2022_tides.py using event windows that match THIS
//    catalogue exactly. The previous table was computed over different
//    windows for 10 of 23 events -- max/range/spring changed materially
//    for may2021, nov2021, may2022, sep2023, oct2023, may2024, jan2025,
//    feb2025 (e.g. sep2023 max 0.884->0.670, spring 1->0).
// 4. Comment fixes: the catalogue holds 23 events, not 19.
// 5. EVENT DATES VERIFIED against news/NADMO/IOM reports (2026-07-22):
//    - nov2021: surge hit at dawn Sun 2021-11-07 (Citi/GNA/WANEP); old
//      window Nov 10-20 started 3 days AFTER the event. Now Nov 6-16.
//    - may2022 RENAMED apr2022: documented Agavedzi/Salakope tidal waves
//      were 2022-04-03/04 (Ghanaian Times/NADMO). Now Apr 3-14.
//    - jan2025: onset 2025-01-16 (GNA). Now Jan 16-26.
//    - feb2025: onset 2025-02-01, 447 displaced (IOM RNA). Now Feb 1-11.
//    - may2024 RENAMED may2025: no May 2024 event is documented anywhere;
//      the documented event is 2025-05-26 (Citi). Now May 24 - Jun 3 2025.
//    - Verified in place: jun2023 (flooding by Jun 11, 3 weeks of rain),
//      sep2023 (surge Wed Sep 20 + Akosombo spill start Sep 15), oct2023
//      (spill peak Oct 10-23, lagoon floodgates opened Oct 23), feb2024
//      (surge early hours Feb 15), mar2025 (surge Mar 1), sep2025_market
//      (market flooded early Fri Sep 12), jun2026_floodgates (mid-June
//      lagoon flooding + Jun 29 2026 rainstorm both inside window).
//    - Unverified by news (kept, confidence MEDIUM): jun/jul 2019, jun/jul
//      2020, may2021, jun2021, jun2022, jul2022, may2026_downpour.
//    Sanity check that fell out of this: every documented TIDAL event now
//    sits on a spring tide in FES2022 (nov2021 0.867m, feb2024 0.884m,
//    mar2025 0.914m, may2025 0.807m) and the rain/dam events sit on neaps.
//
// Datasets verified current in the GEE catalog as of July 2026:
//   COPERNICUS/S1_GRD, COPERNICUS/DEM/GLO30, JRC/GSW1_4/GlobalSurfaceWater,
//   NASA/GPM_L3/IMERG_V07, UCSB-CHG/CHIRPS/DAILY, ECMWF/ERA5/HOURLY
//   (through ~2026-07-16), NASA/SMAP/SPL4SMGP/008.
// =====================================================================

// =====================================================================
// 0. CONFIG -- frozen constants and opt-in behaviour switches
// =====================================================================
// Every number here was, in v4.2, derived implicitly from whatever events
// happened to be in the catalogue. They are now explicit and fixed, so
// adding an event cannot retro-change the meaning of a feature or the
// classification of an already-published event.

// Spring/neap threshold: the MEDIAN tidal range of the original 23-event
// catalogue (2026-07-22). Verified to reproduce all 23 published spring
// flags exactly. Do NOT recompute this from an expanded catalogue --
// keep it pinned and document any deliberate change.
var SPRING_RANGE_THRESHOLD = 1.572;   // metres

// compound_risk tide-range normalisation bounds: the MIN (jun2021) and
// MAX (feb2024) tidal range of the original 23-event catalogue. Pinned
// for the same reason -- if these float, compound_risk values computed
// before and after a catalogue change are not comparable.
var COMPOUND_TIDE_RANGE_MIN = 1.258;  // metres
var COMPOUND_TIDE_RANGE_MAX = 1.793;  // metres

// Fallback constants, named so a reader can see what a "silent default"
// would look like in the exported data.
var SM_FALLBACK          = 0.2;       // volumetric fraction
var PRESSURE_FALLBACK    = 101325;    // Pa (1 atm)
var OTSU_FALLBACK_THRESH = -2.0;      // dB, only if the histogram is null

// --- opt-in switches: BOTH CHANGE THE LABELS. Keep false to reproduce v4.2 ---

// Otsu currently builds its histogram over the whole ROI, including the
// permanent ocean/lagoon, which contributes a large near-zero mode (water
// before AND during) that pulls the between-class split. permWater is
// only applied AFTER thresholding. Setting this true masks permanent
// water before the histogram is built -> different threshold -> different
// flood labels.
var MASK_WATER_BEFORE_OTSU = false;

// The RFI/CII p10/p90 normalisation is currently fitted across ALL 23
// events, so the driver labels of TRAINING events depend on the
// distribution of VALIDATION-period data. Setting this true fits the
// percentiles on training events only -> different driver labels.
var RFICII_PERCENTILES_TRAIN_ONLY = false;

// =====================================================================
// v5.0 LABEL GATES
// =====================================================================
// An event must clear all three or it exports no labelled pixels. Rejecting an
// event is the correct outcome when the SAR pair cannot support a label; a
// catalogue of 12 real events supports a claim, 23 of thresholded noise does not.
// REPORT-ONLY BY DEFAULT. The thresholds below were chosen a priori and are
// almost certainly wrong for this scene: v4.3's own Otsu fallback is -2.0 dB,
// which MIN_DROP_DB = -3.0 would reject. With ENFORCE_LABEL_GATES = false the
// gates are computed and exported per row, but reject nothing, so the observed
// distribution of eta / threshold / extent can be measured first. Set the
// thresholds from that distribution, THEN set this to true and re-export.
var ENFORCE_LABEL_GATES = false;

// Otsu separability is REPORTED but NOT gated. Measured over 24 events it
// runs the wrong way: unverified seasonal proxies median 0.608 against 0.532
// for news-verified events (Mann-Whitney p = 0.046). It does not discriminate.
var MIN_BIMODALITY = 0.0;     // reported only; see qa_otsu_separability

// Set from the measured distribution, not a priori. The flood mask is
// diff < threshold and flooding DARKENS VH, so a meaningful threshold must be
// negative; 11 of 24 events returned a POSITIVE one, and 20 of 24 fell within
// +/-1 dB of zero, which is a threshold splitting noise. At -0.25 dB the eight
// surviving events are all news-verified and none of the eight unverified
// proxies pass (Fisher p = 0.022).
var MIN_DROP_DB    = -0.25;   // required VH darkening, dB
var MAX_DROP_DB    = -25.0;   // beyond this is speckle or a masking artefact
var MAX_FLOOD_FRAC = 0.60;    // plausible upper bound on inundated AOI fraction
var SAMPLES_PER_EVENT = 4000; // was 650, which deduplicated to 268 locations

// Otsu separability eta = between-class variance / total variance. Near 1 means
// two well-separated modes; near 0 means one blob and no defensible threshold.
var otsuSeparability = function(histDict) {
  var h = ee.Dictionary(histDict);
  var counts = ee.Array(h.get("histogram"));
  var means  = ee.Array(h.get("bucketMeans"));
  var size   = means.length().get([0]);
  var total  = counts.reduce(ee.Reducer.sum(), [0]).get([0]);
  var sums   = means.multiply(counts).reduce(ee.Reducer.sum(), [0]).get([0]);
  var mean   = sums.divide(total);
  var totalVar = means.subtract(mean).pow(2).multiply(counts)
                   .reduce(ee.Reducer.sum(), [0]).get([0]).divide(total);
  var bss = ee.List.sequence(1, size.subtract(1)).map(function(i) {
    var aCounts = counts.slice(0, 0, ee.Number(i));
    var aCount  = aCounts.reduce(ee.Reducer.sum(), [0]).get([0]);
    var aMeans  = means.slice(0, 0, ee.Number(i));
    var aMean   = aMeans.multiply(aCounts).reduce(ee.Reducer.sum(), [0]).get([0])
                    .divide(aCount);
    var bCount  = total.subtract(aCount);
    var bMean   = sums.subtract(aMean.multiply(aCount)).divide(bCount);
    return aCount.multiply(aMean.subtract(mean).pow(2))
             .add(bCount.multiply(bMean.subtract(mean).pow(2))).divide(total);
  });
  return ee.Number(ee.List(bss).reduce(ee.Reducer.max())).divide(totalVar);
};

// =====================================================================
// 1. REGION OF INTEREST (Keta Barrier & Lagoon Buffer)
// =====================================================================
var keta = ee.Geometry.Polygon([
  [[0.80, 5.75], [1.15, 5.75], [1.15, 6.10], [0.80, 6.10]]
]);

// Center map
Map.setCenter(0.975, 5.925, 11);
Map.setOptions("HYBRID");

// =====================================================================
// 2. DEM & DEM DERIVATIVES (Copernicus GLO-30, v5.1; was SRTM 30 m)
// =====================================================================
// v5.1 FIX. NASADEM/SRTM is quantised to whole metres: within -1 to 3 m it
// carries exactly THREE distinct values ({0, 1, 2}) across a total water level
// range of 2.94 m. It cannot order cells by exposure inside the band that
// decides inundation, whatever model is fitted.
//
// Measured, identical model, identical spatial blocks, only the DEM changed:
//   NASADEM   out-of-block R2 0.250   seasonal-water residual bias +0.356
//   GLO-30    out-of-block R2 0.466   seasonal-water residual bias +0.229
//   FABDEM    out-of-block R2 0.437   seasonal-water residual bias +0.232
//
// GLO-30 wins. FABDEM strips only ~7.5 cm of canopy on a bare sandy barrier,
// and its ~0.01 m distribution quantisation costs more than the bare-earth
// correction gains here. On a forested floodplain that ranking would reverse.
//
// A mosaicked ImageCollection has no fixed projection, and ee.Terrain needs
// one, so the default projection is set explicitly before slope and aspect.
var srtm = ee.ImageCollection("COPERNICUS/DEM/GLO30").select("DEM").mosaic()
  .setDefaultProjection("EPSG:4326", null, 30);
var dem  = srtm.clip(keta).rename("elevation");
var slope = ee.Terrain.slope(dem).rename("slope");
var aspect = ee.Terrain.aspect(dem).rename("aspect");

// JRC Global Surface Water (permanent water mask + dist_water)
var jrc = ee.Image("JRC/GSW1_4/GlobalSurfaceWater");
var permWater = jrc.select("occurrence").gt(80).unmask(0);
// v5.0 FIX. fastDistanceTransform returns distance to the nearest NON-ZERO
// pixel. v4.3 applied it to permWater.not(), which is distance to the nearest
// LAND: zero on all land, positive only over permanent water. That made the
// band a water mask, not a distance, and because the flood label was forced to
// 0 on permanent water by the same mask, the band DETERMINED the label on 61%
// of exported rows. Model A scored kappa 0.383 by reproducing it; a one-line
// rule scored 0.503; on land alone the model scored 0.032.
var distWater = permWater.fastDistanceTransform(1024).sqrt()
  .multiply(30).rename("dist_water");

// Carry the mask explicitly so downstream code can EXCLUDE water rather than
// silently treat it as dry land.
var isWater = permWater.rename("is_water").toFloat();

// Binary elevation thresholds
var lt3 = dem.lt(3).rename("lt3");
var lt1 = dem.lt(1).rename("lt1");
var lt0 = dem.lt(0).rename("lt0");

// Terrain feature stack
var terrainStack = ee.Image.cat([dem, slope, aspect, distWater, lt3, lt1, lt0, isWater]);

